I chose moderate, opposite‑sign weights for cart position and velocities (negative on position and velocity) to push the cart back toward the centre, and small symmetric weights on the sine terms of the two pole angles (negative for the first pole, positive for the second) so the force reacts to angular deviation in opposite directions, helping to keep both links upright. The cosine terms have low magnitude to avoid large constant offsets, and I set the bias to zero. All weights are modest enough to keep the raw action often within the ±1 range, reducing saturation and chattering while still providing enough corrective force to sustain balance for longer episodes.
